# Indian Dams, Ports, Airports, and Power Plants

> Questions on "which dam is on which river" or "which port is the only
> riverine one" turn up in almost every SSC, RRB and state-police
> paper. The facts are simple, but the distractors are close
> neighbours: Bhakra versus Tehri, Kandla versus Kolkata, Tarapur versus
> Kudankulam. This lesson gives the river, state and one hook for each.

## 1. Major Dams: River and State

[definition]
A **multipurpose river valley project** uses one dam for several jobs at
once: irrigation, hydroelectric power, flood control, drinking water and
sometimes navigation or fisheries. Jawaharlal Nehru called such dams the
"temples of modern India".
[/definition]

| Dam | River | State | Exam hook |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Tehri | Bhagirathi (near its meeting with the Bhilangana) | Uttarakhand | Tallest dam in India, about 260.5 m; rock and earth fill |
| Bhakra | Sutlej | Himachal Pradesh | Reservoir is **Gobind Sagar**; about 226 m, a very high concrete gravity dam |
| Hirakud | Mahanadi | Odisha | Among the longest earthen dams in the world; completed 1957 |
| Nagarjuna Sagar | Krishna | Telangana and Andhra Pradesh border | A huge masonry dam; completed 1967 |
| Srisailam | Krishna | Andhra Pradesh and Telangana border | Large hydropower station |
| Sardar Sarovar | Narmada | Gujarat | Inaugurated in 2017; Kevadia, near the Statue of Unity |
| Indira Sagar | Narmada | Madhya Pradesh | Large reservoir upstream of Sardar Sarovar |
| Koyna | Koyna | Maharashtra | Called the lifeline of Maharashtra for power |
| Idukki | Periyar | Kerala | Double-curvature **arch dam** |
| Mettur | Cauvery | Tamil Nadu | Reservoir named Stanley Reservoir; built 1934 |
| Tungabhadra | Tungabhadra | Karnataka (shared with Andhra Pradesh) | Completed 1953 |
| Rihand | Rihand | Uttar Pradesh | Reservoir named Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar |
| Pong | Beas | Himachal Pradesh | Reservoir named Maharana Pratap Sagar |
| Ukai | Tapi | Gujarat | Tapi's main dam |
| Salal and Baglihar | Chenab | Jammu and Kashmir | Hydropower projects |

- **Chambal Valley** has three dams: **Gandhi Sagar** (Madhya Pradesh),
  **Rana Pratap Sagar** and **Jawahar Sagar** (both Rajasthan).
- **Kallanai (the Grand Anicut)** on the Cauvery in Tamil Nadu dates to the
  Chola king **Karikalan** (about the 2nd century CE), and is among the
  oldest water-regulation structures still in use.
- **Damodar Valley Corporation (1948)** was modelled on America's
  Tennessee Valley Authority. The Damodar was once known as the "Sorrow of
  Bengal" for its floods.
- The **Farakka Barrage** (West Bengal, on the Ganga, working from 1975)
  sends water down the Bhagirathi-Hooghly to flush silt from **Kolkata
  port**.

## 2. Major Ports of India

India's coast is served by a list of long-standing **12 major ports**
(owned by the Union government) and a large number of minor and private
ports. Six lie on the east coast and six on the west.

| Port | State | Coast | Exam hook |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Syama Prasad Mookerjee Port (Kolkata, with Haldia dock) | West Bengal | East | Only **riverine** major port (Hooghly); renamed 2020 |
| Paradip | Odisha | East | Deep-water port serving iron ore and coal |
| Visakhapatnam | Andhra Pradesh | East | Natural harbour |
| Kamarajar Port (Ennore) | Tamil Nadu | East | Coal and bulk port |
| Chennai | Tamil Nadu | East | **Artificial** harbour |
| V.O. Chidambaranar (Tuticorin) | Tamil Nadu | East | Renamed in 2011; artificial, deep-water |
| Cochin (Kochi) | Kerala | West | Natural harbour; international transshipment role |
| New Mangalore | Karnataka | West | Declared a major port in 1974; iron ore export |
| Mormugao | Goa | West | One of the oldest west-coast ports |
| Mumbai | Maharashtra | West | Big natural harbour |
| Jawaharlal Nehru Port (Nhava Sheva) | Maharashtra | West | Commissioned 1989; chief container port among the major ports |
| Deendayal Port (Kandla) | Gujarat | West | **Tidal** port on the Gulf of Kutch; renamed 2017 |

[tip]
Memory trick: Tamil Nadu has three east-coast majors (Ennore, Chennai,
Tuticorin) and Maharashtra has two on the west (Mumbai and JNPA).
Kolkata is the only river port; Kandla is the famous tidal port.
[/tip]

- **Mundra** (Gujarat) is a **private** port, run by Adani, and is the
  country's largest port by cargo handled, so it is not one of the 12
  government major ports.
- **Vadhavan** (near Dahanu, Palghar district, Maharashtra) was approved by
  the Union Cabinet on **19 June 2024** as a greenfield deep-draft major
  port, still being developed. It is the port to watch for "new port"
  questions.

## 3. Airports and Their Official Names

Many airports carry a person's name, and exams ask the reverse: "Airport X is
in which city?"

| Airport | City | State or UT |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Indira Gandhi International | Delhi | Delhi |
| Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj International | Mumbai | Maharashtra |
| Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose International | Kolkata | West Bengal |
| Kempegowda International | Bengaluru | Karnataka |
| Rajiv Gandhi International | Hyderabad | Telangana |
| Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel International | Ahmedabad | Gujarat |
| Lokpriya Gopinath Bordoloi International | Guwahati | Assam |
| Veer Savarkar International | Port Blair | Andaman and Nicobar Islands |
| Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar International | Nagpur | Maharashtra |
| Chaudhary Charan Singh International | Lucknow | Uttar Pradesh |
| Lal Bahadur Shastri | Varanasi | Uttar Pradesh |
| Biju Patnaik | Bhubaneswar | Odisha |
| Sri Guru Ram Dass Jee International | Amritsar | Punjab |
| Manohar International (Mopa) | Mopa | Goa |

- **Cochin International Airport** (Kerala) opened in **1999** and was the
  first Indian airport built under a **public-private partnership** model.
  In 2015 it became the first airport in the world to run fully on solar
  power.
- Airport names change by government order, so check the date in any
  "latest" question.

## 4. Power Plants by Type

| Type | Plant | State | Hook |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Thermal (coal) | Vindhyachal (NTPC) | Madhya Pradesh | Among the largest coal stations in the country |
| Thermal (coal) | Korba, Singrauli, Talcher, Farakka (NTPC) | Chhattisgarh, UP/MP, Odisha, West Bengal | Coalfield-based stations |
| Thermal (lignite) | Neyveli | Tamil Nadu | Runs on lignite |
| Nuclear | Tarapur | Maharashtra | First nuclear power station (1969) |
| Nuclear | Rawatbhata (Kota) | Rajasthan | Rajasthan Atomic Power Station |
| Nuclear | Kalpakkam | Tamil Nadu | Madras Atomic Power Station; fast-breeder programme |
| Nuclear | Narora, Kakrapar, Kaiga | UP, Gujarat, Karnataka | Pressurised heavy-water reactors |
| Nuclear | Kudankulam | Tamil Nadu | Russian-designed reactors; India's largest nuclear station |
| Hydro | Nathpa Jhakri | Himachal Pradesh | On the Sutlej; 1,500 MW |
| Hydro | Tehri | Uttarakhand | 1,000 MW first-stage hydro station on the Bhagirathi |
| Solar | Bhadla | Rajasthan | Among the largest solar parks |
| Solar | Pavagada | Karnataka | Large solar park in Tumakuru district |
| Wind | Muppandal | Tamil Nadu | Large wind farm near Kanyakumari |

- The **Haryana** plants that appear in state exams are Panipat, Khedar
  (Hisar), Yamunanagar and Jhajjar (all thermal).
- The **International Solar Alliance** is headquartered in **Gurugram**,
  Haryana.

## 5. Common Traps

- **Bhakra versus Tehri**: Bhakra (Sutlej, Himachal) is a concrete gravity
  dam; Tehri (Bhagirathi, Uttarakhand) is the tallest dam. Do not swap them.
- **Hirakud (Mahanadi, Odisha)** versus **Nagarjuna Sagar (Krishna)**: one
  is earthen, the other masonry.
- **Sardar Sarovar is in Gujarat; Indira Sagar is in Madhya Pradesh**, both
  on the Narmada.
- **Kolkata is a river port; Kandla is a tidal port; Mundra is private.**
- **Tarapur is the oldest nuclear plant; Kudankulam is the biggest.**

[example]
Question: "Which Indian dam stands on the Bhagirathi river?" Think of the
Bhagirathi as the headstream of the Ganga in Uttarakhand, so the dam is
Tehri. If the river is the Sutlej, think Bhakra, and the state flips to
Himachal Pradesh.
[/example]

[summary]
Tehri (Bhagirathi, Uttarakhand) is India's tallest dam, Bhakra (Sutlej)
holds Gobind Sagar, Hirakud is on the Mahanadi and Nagarjuna Sagar on the
Krishna. Of the 12 major ports, Kolkata is the only riverine port,
Kandla is tidal, Chennai and Tuticorin are artificial harbours, and
Mundra is private. Airports are named after leaders, so memorise the
city of each name. Tarapur began nuclear power in 1969, Kudankulam is the
largest nuclear station, and Vindhyachal is a giant coal plant in Madhya
Pradesh.
[/summary]
